    "following_feedback_interpretation": "The previous outcomes support a genuine late-elution tail but weaken my earlier tendency to associate high spread and heteroatom-rich or basic structures with late onset. m177 b514312eda7950cdcf1c measured 4.49/81.30 mL despite historical predictions 59.61/139.73, establishing early onset with delayed completion. Dibenzylamine d5234e966aa231be05e5 and sulfur-containing aldehyde 4c46ceefd6147b1e0a7a also exposed large overpredictions. Conversely, pyridyl ester 96710afeabc594baa359, multifunctional ester ca467c94f14b2219503e and trimethoxybenzene b070c3657459c06a4df1 were underpredicted. Nitro-carbonyl rows a5f9179bfb146ad70953 and b6f1b33a5491968c645e had much larger historical completion errors than onset errors, motivating separate response constraints for both targets. These are acquisition-time errors, not current-model residuals. The m159 and m165 outcomes strengthen molecule-specific solvent-response reasoning, while the available loading comparisons do not establish a universal loading law. In particular, a917c928912c74862968 has V1 slightly above V2 and should not be treated as clean mechanistic evidence. I also correct the earlier functional-group description: m26 is a beta-diketone, not an ester. Quantile widths remain auxiliary model-behavior signals; zero V2 lower quantiles and quantile crossing limit their interpretation as uncertainty.",
